WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.275 --> 00:00:02.020
MAX SALTONSTALL: Welcome to
"BeyondCorp in a Bottle,"

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:03.686
where we explore
journeys into the world

00:00:03.686 --> 00:00:06.378
of context-aware access
and zero trust networking.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.020
As your modern applications
evolve and grow,

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.480
you end up with a set of
services, apps, deployments,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.880
and resources that need to
interact with each other,

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.920
often across disparate networks.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.930
These tend to grow
organically, and that quickly

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.630
gets messy, or
downright unmanageable.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.704
Let's look at EatAnd.Run,
a small startup,

00:00:26.704 --> 00:00:28.620
catering to joggers,
hikers, and power walkers

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.164
who get peckish on the go.

00:00:30.164 --> 00:00:31.830
They've been going
quickly since launch,

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.288
and have some pretty personal
information about the clients

00:00:34.288 --> 00:00:35.790
that they need to secure.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.740
They knew that fully
open access wouldn't

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:38.910
work for their systems.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.100
Too high risk of leaks.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.960
Adding layers of firewall rules
quickly became unmanageable,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.170
so they turned to
Identity Aware Proxy,

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.590
along with identity and access
management to solve this.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.020
After they set up some
basic IAP access controls,

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.890
they wanted to go further.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.250
The product team got excited
about new social sharing

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:57.807
features being
developed in the app,

00:00:57.807 --> 00:00:59.640
and they needed to keep
these features quiet

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.890
until they were
ready for prime time.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.690
So they pushed a new version
of the App Engine back in live.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.050
We used IAP to restrict
it to just the engineering

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.120
team for internal testing.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.340
To further protect the
innovations they had brewing,

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.560
the EatAnd.Run security team
made the development version

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.110
of their web app restricted to
people using up to date Chrome

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.990
OS devices with a screen
lock, so no data might

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.410
leak inadvertently through
snooping or lost laptops.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.060
These sorts of
access policies can

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.790
be defined quickly in
Access Context Manager,

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.285
or via a YAML file.

00:01:28.285 --> 00:01:30.660
The EatAnd.Run team realized
that a binary policy was too

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.600
simplistic for their needs, so
they decided to set up three

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:34.470
trust tiers--

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:35.919
breakfast, lunch, and supper.

00:01:35.919 --> 00:01:38.460
These access levels are based
on the status of the employee's

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.250
operating system, screen
lock, and disk encryption,

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.380
and help IAP decide
whether or not

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.090
a given request gets
to the protected apps.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.450
Now the EatAnd.Run team can
enjoy some peace of mind,

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.090
knowing that only the right
people on secure devices

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.492
will be able to play
with these new features.

00:01:53.492 --> 00:01:55.200
Take a look at the
blog post linked below

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.570
to see more details on how
the team set up these access

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.270
controls and figure out if you
qualify for breakfast or lunch

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.530
at EatAnd.Run.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:02.590
Thanks for watching.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.660
Subscribe here to get more
videos on context-aware access.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.880
When it comes to protecting your
company, keep your data yours.

